---
import IllustrationFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/IllustrationFigure.astro"
import SvgFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/SvgFigure.astro"
import { series, stroke } from "~/data/palette"

const stars = [
  { r: 32, count: 6 },
  { r: 66, count: 24 },
  { r: 100, count: 54 },
]

// Further shells are only partly visible and fade toward the edge of the illustration.
const continuingShells = [
  { r: 134, count: 96, opacity: 0.34, dotRadius: 1 },
  { r: 168, count: 150, opacity: 0.17, dotRadius: 0.8 },
]
---

<IllustrationFigure
  name="olbers-paradox"
  lead="Farther shells contain more stars, offsetting how much dimmer each star looks."
>
  <SvgFigure
    name="olbers-shells"
    class="mx-auto w-full max-w-lg"
    viewBox="0 0 500 310"
    title="Star shells contributing equal light in an ideal eternal universe"
    desc="Three complete concentric shells of stars surround an observer. Two additional, progressively fainter shells of stars are partly cropped to suggest that the shells continue indefinitely. Successively larger shells contain more stars, while individual stars become dimmer. Three equal bars show that each complete shell contributes about the same amount of light. The model is an ideal static eternal universe, with no horizon or edge of space."
  >
    <defs>
      <clipPath id="olbers-continuing-shells-clip">
        <rect x="0" y="36" width="245" height="244" />
      </clipPath>
    </defs>
    <!-- Partial shells vanish into the diagram's edges; the clip is not a cosmic boundary. -->
    <g clip-path="url(#olbers-continuing-shells-clip)">
      {continuingShells.map(({ r, count, opacity, dotRadius }) => (
        <g opacity={opacity}>
          <circle cx="132" cy="143" r={r} fill="none" stroke={stroke} />
          {Array.from({ length: count }, (_, i) => {
            const angle = (2 * Math.PI * i) / count
            return (
              <circle
                cx={132 + r * Math.cos(angle)}
                cy={143 + r * Math.sin(angle)}
                r={dotRadius}
                fill={series.gold}
              />
            )
          })}
        </g>
      ))}
    </g>
    <g fill="none" stroke={stroke}>
      <circle cx="132" cy="143" r="32" stroke-opacity=".45" />
      <circle cx="132" cy="143" r="66" stroke-opacity=".45" />
      <circle cx="132" cy="143" r="100" stroke-opacity=".45" />
    </g>
    {stars.map(({ r, count }, index) => (
      <g>
        {Array.from({ length: count }, (_, i) => {
          const angle = (2 * Math.PI * i) / count
          return (
            <circle
              cx={132 + r * Math.cos(angle)}
              cy={143 + r * Math.sin(angle)}
              r={index === 0 ? 4.3 : index === 1 ? 2.2 : 1.3}
              fill={series.gold}
            />
          )
        })}
      </g>
    ))}
    <circle cx="132" cy="143" r="7" fill={series.blue} stroke={stroke} stroke-width="1.5" />
    <g fill="currentColor" font-size="14">
      <text x="132" y="22" text-anchor="middle">Observer at centre</text>
      <text x="265" y="31" font-weight="700">Ideal eternal universe</text>
      <text x="265" y="57">Same total light from</text>
      <text x="265" y="75">each shell</text>
    </g>
    {[0, 1, 2].map((i) => (
      <g>
        <text x="265" y={115 + i * 35} fill="currentColor" font-size="14">Shell {i + 1}</text>
        <rect x="336" y={101 + i * 35} width="90" height="16" fill={series.gold} fill-opacity=".7" />
      </g>
    ))}
    <g fill="currentColor" font-size="14">
      <text x="265" y="227">Shell 4, shell 5, …</text>
      <text x="265" y="246">continue without end</text>
    </g>
  </SvgFigure>
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    In the imagined eternal, unchanging universe, each equally thick shell would add
    about the same light. With shells extending without end, the whole sky would be bright.
    In reality, light has had only finite time to reach us, stars have finite lifetimes,
    and expansion also reduces observed light. Schematic, not to scale.
  </Fragment>
</IllustrationFigure>